## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to extend Dijkstra's algorithm to count shortest paths while handling ties and modular arithmetic—a common pattern in competitive programming and graph algorithms.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm with path counting augmentation
- Non-negative edge weights, including zero-weight edges
- Modular arithmetic for large path counts and overflow prevention
- Edge case handling: unreachable destinations, multiple optimal paths, zero-weight cycles

## Code Example
```javascript
function countShortestPaths(n, edges, s, t) {
  const MOD = 1e9 + 7;
  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = new Array(n).fill(0);

  dist[s] = 0;
  ways[s] = 1;

  const pq = new MinPriorityQueue({ priority: x => dist[x] });
  pq.enqueue(s);

  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
    const u = pq.dequeue().element;

    for (const [v, w] of edges[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        ways[v] = ways[u];
        pq.enqueue(v);
      } else if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) {
        ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }

  return { distance: dist[t] === Infinity ? -1 : dist[t], paths: ways[t] };
}
```

## Time & Space Complexity
- **Time**: O((N + M) log N) due to priority queue operations
- **Space**: O(N) for distance and path count arrays

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your understanding of counting DP, covering both memoization (top-down) and tabulation (bottom-up) approaches, along with space optimization and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts

- Subset sum counting with each element used at most once
- Memoization (top-down) with recursive DFS and memo table
- Tabulation (bottom-up) with 1D space optimization to O(S)
- Modulo arithmetic to prevent integer overflow

## Edge Cases

- **Empty subset (N=0)**: If N=0 and S=0, return 1 (one valid subset - empty set). If S>0, return 0.
- **Target S=0**: Always return 1 (the empty subset counts as one valid subset).
- **All elements greater than S**: No valid subsets exist, return 0.

## Code Example

```javascript
function countSubsets(nums, target) {
  const mod = 1_000_000_007;
  const n = nums.length;

  // Top-down with memoization
  const memo = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(target + 1).fill(-1));
  function dfs(i, s) {
    if (s === 0) return 1;
    if (i === 0) return 0;
    if (memo[i][s] !== -1) return memo[i][s];
    memo[i][s] = dfs(i - 1, s) % mod;
    if (s >= nums[i - 1]) {
      memo[i][s] = (memo[i][s] + dfs(i - 1, s - nums[i - 1])) % mod;
    }
    return memo[i][s];
  }
  return dfs(n, target);
}

// Bottom-up with space optimization
function countSubsetsBU(nums, target) {
  const mod = 1_000_000_007;
  const dp = Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;

  for (const num of nums) {
    for (let s = target; s >= num; s--) {
      dp[s] = (dp[s] + dp[s - num]) % mod;
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

Both solutions run in O(N×S) time with the bottom-up approach using O(S) space.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash tables with heaps for real-time top-K queries under sliding-window expirations. It evaluates understanding of lazy cleanup techniques, time-based eviction strategies, and maintaining data consistency under concurrent updates.

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for O(1) task lookups and metadata storage
- Max-heap for efficient top-K ordering
- Lazy deletion to avoid expensive per-update heap removals
- Time-based expiration using timestamp validation

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map(); // task_id -> {score, timestamp}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // (score, task_id)
  }

  update(task_id, delta, ts) {
    const current = this.map.get(task_id) || { score: 0, timestamp: 0 };
    const updated = { 
      score: current.score + delta, 
      timestamp: ts 
    };
    this.map.set(task_id, updated);
    this.heap.push([updated.score, task_id]);
  }

  getTopK(k, current_ts) {
    const result = [];
    const temp = [];

    while (result.length < k && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [score, task_id] = this.heap.pop();
      const task = this.map.get(task_id);

      if (task && task.score === score && current_ts - task.timestamp <= this.W) {
        result.push({ task_id, score });
      } else {
        temp.push([score, task_id]);
      }
    }

    // Restore invalid entries
    temp.forEach(entry => this.heap.push(entry));

    return result;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Update**: O(1) hash map + O(log n) heap push = O(log n)
- **Top-K query**: O(k log n) worst case due to lazy deletion
- **Space**: O(n) for hash map + heap (potentially O(n + m) where m are stale entries)
- **Expiration**: Handled during queries via timestamp validation

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical sorting algorithm design that balances cache efficiency with worst-case performance guarantees. Hybrid approaches like this are commonly used in real-world systems where both average-case speed and theoretical bounds matter.

## Key Concepts
- **Hybrid sorting**: Combines partitioning (QuickSort) with stable merging (MergeSort)
- **Introsort-like depth limiting**: Prevents pathological cases
- **In-place stable merging**: Achieved through array rotations
- **Cache-friendly recursive structure**: Larger partitions processed first
- **Complexity**: Average/worst case O(n log n), additional space O(log n) for recursion stack

## Code Implementation
```javascript
function hybridSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1, depth = 0) {
  const n = right - left + 1;
  const threshold = 32;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  if (n <= 1) return;

  if (n <= threshold || depth >= maxDepth) {
    // Bottom-up in-place stable merge sort
    mergeSortInPlace(arr, left, right);
    return;
  }

  // QuickSort with median-of-three pivot
  const pivotIndex = medianOfThree(arr, left, right);
  const partitionIndex = partition(arr, left, right, pivotIndex);

  // Recurse on larger partition first to bound stack depth
  const leftSize = partitionIndex - left;
  const rightSize = right - partitionIndex;

  if (leftSize > rightSize) {
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
  } else {
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you empirically determine the optimal threshold size?
- What techniques would you use to verify the algorithm's stability?
- How does this hybrid approach perform on partially sorted data?
- What modifications would be needed for parallel execution?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of a practical data structure for real-world features (auto-complete) and how to extend it with per-word frequencies.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequency tracking
- DFS/BFS to collect candidate words from a prefix
- Sorting by frequency then lexicographic order
- Edge cases: missing prefix, zero matches, tie handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.freq = 0; // frequency for word ending here
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.freq += 1;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (n, path) => {
      if (n.freq > 0) results.push([path, n.freq]);
      for (const [ch, child] of n.next) dfs(child, path + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, prefix);
    results.sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
    return results.slice(0, K).map(v => v[0]);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you prune rarely-used branches to save memory?
- How would you support concurrent inserts and autocomplete in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked

Standard quicksort performs well on average but can degrade to O(n²) on adversarial inputs, particularly with many duplicates. Introsort-style hybrids combine the practical efficiency of quicksort with worst-case guarantees, making-world engineering this a common real constraint.

## Key Concepts

- **3-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)**: Partitions array into elements less than, equal to, and greater than the pivot, dramatically reducing comparisons for datasets with many duplicates
- **Recursion depth tracking**: Prevents stack overflow and performance degradation on pathological inputs
- **Hybrid switching**: Falls back to mergesort or heapsort when depth exceeds 2*log2(N) or partition size drops below a threshold (~16 elements)
- **Worst-case guarantees**: Ensures O(n log n) time complexity regardless of input distribution

## Code Example

```javascript
function hybridSort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  function quickSort3Way(lo, hi, depth) {
    if (hi - lo < 16) {
      insertionSort(arr, lo, hi);
      return;
    }
    if (depth > maxDepth) {
      mergeSort(arr, lo, hi);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = partition3Way(arr, lo, hi);
    quickSort3Way(lo, lt - 1, depth + 1);
    quickSort3Way(gt + 1, hi, depth + 1);
  }

  quickSort3Way(0, n - 1, 0);
  return arr;
}
```

